  "name": "The Illumination",
  "aka": [],
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "In the year 994, the Eternal Light descended upon every living soul across the known world who could trace at least one grandparent to the Moscavian bloodline, seizing them in a blinding radiance and transforming their bodies into those of great owls while leaving their human minds, memories, and personalities wholly intact.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Church of the Eternal Light interprets this event as the supreme revelation of divine will—the Eternal Light's ultimate act of illumination, raising Moscavia's children to a higher state of being.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": "Church of the Eternal Light"
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The transformation did not destroy the Tsardom of Moscavia but rather reshaped it, as the newly formed Owl-folk reorganized governance, adapted architecture, and continued the work of civilization under the reigning Tsar.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The event is regarded by the faithful as proof of the Eternal Light's power and love for the Moscavian people, a sacred act that elevated them above the limitations of mortal flesh.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": "Church of the Eternal Light"
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "In the years following the Illumination, the Zakonnik—the Tsardom's legal code—was revised to address the practical needs of an owl-bodied society, including regulations on roosting rights, territorial disputes over elevated nesting spaces, and the scheduling of commerce to align with nocturnal preferences.",
      "since": 996,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Illumination unified the Moscavian people in a profound way, as every citizen of the Tsardom—regardless of station, wealth, or prior status—was transformed together, creating a shared experience of divine elevation.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Parish churches across Moscavia serve as living reminders of the Illumination, their dim, candlelit interiors designed to accommodate the nocturnal sensibilities of the Owl-folk while maintaining the sacred atmosphere of worship.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The event has become the central narrative of Moscavian identity and faith, taught to young Owl-folk from the moment of their baptism by candlelight and reinforced throughout their spiritual education.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": "Church of the Eternal Light"
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Non-Moscavian populations who had intermarried with Moscavian bloodlines were also transformed, though their offspring—the eared owl-folk—bore physical differences reflecting their mixed heritage, complicating diplomatic relations with neighboring kingdoms.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Illumination remains a subject of theological study and devotion within the Church, explored through sermons, iconography, and the annual Festival of the Solstice Flame, when the great bonfires lit across every parish square symbolize the original light that changed the world.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": "Church of the Eternal Light"
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Six years have passed since the Illumination, and the year is now 1000, a decade into the new age of the Owl-folk, during which the Tsardom has proven its resilience and the Church has deepened its doctrinal reflections on the meaning of divine transformation.",
      "since": 1000,
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
