  "name": "The Divine Right of the Tsar",
  "aka": [],
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Tsar of Moscavia rules as the earthly steward of the Eternal Light, governing the Tsardom by divine right conferred through the blessing of the Church of the Eternal Light.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The concept holds that the Eternal Light, as the supreme source of creation and divine order, has chosen the Tsar and Tsarina of Moscavia to serve as its chosen rulers on earth, entrusted with the stewardship of the realm and its people.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "This doctrine is rooted in the founding of the Tsardom of Moscavia, when the first Tsar declared the Eternal Light the patron deity of the realm and established the Church of the Eternal Light as the state religion.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Blinding Transformation of 994, which converted every person of Moscavian bloodline into Owl-folk, was interpreted by the Church as the Eternal Light's ultimate revelation and a confirmation of the Tsar's sacred mandate.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Tsar's authority is considered inseparable from the Church's blessing; without the Church's recognition of divine sanction, no ruler may claim the throne of Moscavia with legitimate authority.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Patriarch of Moscavia, as the supreme head of the Church, plays a ceremonial but symbolically vital role in the coronation of each new Tsar or Tsarina, affirming the Eternal Light's continued blessing upon the dynasty.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The doctrine does not merely grant the Tsar political authority but frames governance itself as a sacred duty — the Tsar is expected to rule with justice and piety, as a reflection of the Eternal Light's will upon the earth.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Dissent from the Divine Right doctrine is treated as both political treason and spiritual heresy within the Tsardom, as it challenges both the civil and ecclesiastical order simultaneously.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The doctrine is codified in the Zakonnik, the Tsardom's legal code, which enshrines the Tsar's divine authority as the foundational principle of Moscavian law.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "In practice, the Tsar wields both legislative and judicial power, with the Church serving as the moral and spiritual arbiter of the realm's governance, ensuring that secular authority operates within the framework of divine law.",
      "since": null,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Divine Right of the Tsar has survived the transformation of the Moscavian people into Owl-folk, with the doctrine adapting seamlessly to the changed physical nature of both ruler and ruled.",
      "since": 994,
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
