{ "name": "The Runeweavers' Oath",
  "aka": [
    {
      "name": "The Weaver's Vow",
      "tag": "common",
      "everyday": true
    },
    {
      "name": "The Binding Words",
      "tag": "common",
      "everyday": false
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Every aspiring Runeweaver must swear the Oath upon completion of their apprenticeship and before they are admitted to the craft as a full practitioner.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Oath is spoken aloud before the assembled Conclave at Stonehold, with the weaver's hands resting on an unmarked stone called the Witness.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Oath binds the weaver to serve the craft with integrity, to never inscribe a rune for profit alone, and to respect the material upon which they work.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "A Runeweaver who breaks the Oath is subject to trial by the Conclave and, if found guilty, may be stripped of their rank and exiled from all sanctioned practice.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The original text of the Oath was written by the Conclave's first Speaker, Aldric of Greyholm, and was among the works destroyed in the Burning of 888.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The current recitation of the Oath is maintained through oral tradition and three surviving partial transcripts, each housed in different Conclave libraries.",
      "since": 888,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Oath includes a clause acknowledging that the weaver's own life force may be called upon to fuel an inscription, and that this sacrifice must be made freely, never coerced.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Apprentices who refuse to swear the Oath are dismissed from training and forbidden from practicing Runework in any Conclave-recognized territory.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Oath does not expire; once sworn, it holds for the weaver's entire life. There is no known ritual to release a weaver from its binding.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Weavers who travel to the southern kingdoms sometimes find that local customs allow minor deviations from the Oath's strictures, a point of ongoing debate within the Conclave.",
      "since": 950,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The Witness stone upon which the Oath is sworn is said to be a fragment of limestone taken from the walls of Ashenmere, though this claim has never been verified.",
      "since": 642,
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
  "names": [
    {
      "name": "Aldric of Greyholm",
      "kind": "character",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "The Witness",
      "kind": "item",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "The Conclave",
      "kind": "order",
      "aka": [],
      "template": "order",
      "part_of": {
        "entry": "Runeweaving",
        "drawer": "Doctrines"
      }
    }
  ],
  "elsewhere": [
    {
      "entry": "Stonehold",
      "text": "Stonehold serves as the site where every Runeweaver swears the Oath, making it the spiritual heart of the craft's covenant.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "The Doctrine of Cost",
      "text": "The Oath reinforces the Doctrine of Cost by requiring weavers to acknowledge that every inscription demands a genuine exchange of energy.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "The Prohibition of the Flesh Rune",
      "text": "The Prohibition is codified within the Oath itself, making it one of the three foundational promises a weaver must speak aloud.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "The Law of Resonance",
      "text": "The Oath requires weavers to affirm their understanding of the Law of Resonance, ensuring they approach every inscription with knowledge of the material's nature.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "Ashenmere",
      "text": "The Oath's connection to Ashenmere is maintained through the tradition of the Witness stone, linking the oldest known runes to the oldest known vow.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "The Greyholm Archive",
      "text": "The destruction of the Greyholm Archive in 888 forced the Conclave to rely on memory and scattered fragments to preserve the exact wording of the Oath.",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
  "part_of": {
    "entry": "Runeweaving",
    "drawer": "Doctrines"
  },
  "move_in": ["M5"]
}

{ "name": "The Ashenmere Caves",
  "aka": [
    {
      "name": "The Ashen Caves",
      "tag": "common",
      "everyday": true
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "description",
      "text": "The Ashenmere Caves are a vast network of limestone caves and subterranean galleries carved into the northern foothills of the Greymarch Mountains, stretching across roughly twelve square miles of twisting passages, open chambers, and deep vertical shafts.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "description",
      "text": "The cave walls are coated in a fine layer of volcanic ash — the source of the 'ashen' in the caves' name — deposited over millennia by eruptions from the Greymarch caldera to the south, which has long since gone dormant.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "description",
      "text": "The air inside is cool and still, carrying a faint mineral scent mixed with the musty smell of ancient stone. Water drips through stalactites in some chambers, while others are bone-dry, their floors cracked and pale as old bone.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "The first systematic excavation began in 947, when a Runeweaver named Maren Ashwick discovered a chamber near the eastern entrance covered floor to ceiling in intact runic inscriptions dating back at least two thousand years.",
      "since": "947",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "Since 947, approximately one-third of the cave system has been mapped and accessible to scholars, though only a fraction of the inscriptions have been fully transcribed and analyzed.",
      "since": "947",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "history",
      "text": "Before 947, local shepherds and miners had known about the caves for generations but regarded them as dangerous and unremarkable, never venturing past the first few chambers.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The caves contain evidence of at least four distinct periods of rune inscription, spanning from crude, deeply carved glyphs in the oldest chambers to finely detailed etchings in the more recently used passages.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Some chambers appear to have been deliberately sealed off by the ancient weavers themselves, using inscribed stone barriers that resist any attempt to break them apart — a technique not yet fully understood by modern weavers.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The oldest inscriptions in the deepest chambers predate the First Codex of Aelberth by over a millennium and employ glyphs and syntactic structures found nowhere else in the known rune tradition.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The site is managed by a permanent research settlement of roughly forty weavers and scholars, known informally as the Ashenmere Circle, who live in prefabricated stone shelters built near the main entrance.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Access to the inner chambers is restricted to weavers of at least journeyman rank, and all visitors must swear an oath of non-interference with the inscriptions — touching the walls without authorization is strictly forbidden.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The caves have yielded several fragments of what scholars believe may be a pre-Aelberthic rune grammar, potentially rewriting the understanding of how the art was practiced in its earliest centuries.",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
  "names": [
    {
      "name": "Maren Ashwick",
      "kind": "character",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "The Ashenmere Circle",
      "kind": "order",
      "aka": [],
      "template": "order"
    },
    {
      "name": "The Greymarch Mountains",
      "kind": "place",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "Greyholm",
      "kind": "place",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "Stonehold",
      "kind": "place",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "The Runeweavers' Conclave",
      "kind": "order",
      "aka": [],
      "template": "order"
    },
    {
      "name": "Aelberth",
      "kind": "character",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "The First Codex of Aelberth",
      "kind": "item",
      "aka": []
    }
  ],
  "elsewhere": [
    {
      "entry": "Runeweaving",
      "text": "The Ashenmere Caves are recognized as the single most important archaeological site in the entire rune tradition, and their ongoing excavation is considered the most significant effort to recover knowledge lost during the Burning of Greyholm in 888.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "Deepcarving",
      "text": "Many of the Ashenmere inscriptions appear to have been created using a form of deepcarving — runes etched well below the surface of the limestone — which accounts for their extraordinary durability over thousands of years.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "The Greyholm Archive",
      "text": "The destruction of the Greyholm Archive in 888 made the Ashenmere discoveries all the more critical, as the caves now hold what may be the largest surviving body of pre-888 rune knowledge.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "The Doctrine of Cost",
      "text": "The inscriptions found in Ashenmere suggest that the ancient weavers understood the Doctrine of Cost in a more nuanced way than modern weavers do, with some glyphs appearing to draw energy from the stone itself rather than from the weaver or the material alone.",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
  "part_of": {
    "entry": "Runeweaving",
    "drawer": "Sites"
  },
  "move_in": ["M3", "M7"]
}

{ "name": "The Festival of the Solstice Flame",
  "aka": [],
  "facts": [
    {
      "category": "summary",
      "text": "On the longest night of winter, every parish in Moscavia lights a great bonfire in its central square as part of the Church of the Eternal Light's annual celebration of the Solstice Flame.",
      "since": 412,
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The festival marks the turning point of the year, when the nights begin to shorten and the Eternal Light's influence is believed to grow stronger over the darkness.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Each bonfire is lit at dusk from a single flame carried by a parish priest, who kindles it using a taper traced back to the great hearth in the Cathedral of the Eternal Light in Moskov.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Owl-folk gather in their parish squares throughout the night, singing hymns, sharing food, and bearing witness to the Light's enduring presence even at the darkest time of year.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The bonfire is built from twelve logs, each one representing one of the months of the year, with the center log left unlit until midnight — the symbolic heart of winter — when the patriarch in Moskov signals its kindling.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Children of the parish are given small candles at the festival's end, which they carry home to light their family hearths, extending the Light's blessing into every household.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Parish priests are responsible for organizing the festival locally, preparing the bonfire site, arranging the hymn-singing, and ensuring the flame is carried properly from the cathedral chain.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "In Moskov itself, the bonfire in the great square before the Cathedral of the Eternal Light is lit by the Patriarch in a ceremony attended by the Tsar and Tsarina, the Synod of Lanterns, and thousands of Owl-folk from across the realm.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "The festival serves as a reminder that the Eternal Light never truly wanes, even in the longest darkness, and that the Owl-folk's transformation was the Light's promise that no shadow can overcome its radiance.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "general",
      "text": "Villages and towns that lack a formal parish church still observe the festival, gathering around bonfires in their market squares with the blessing of the nearest parish priest.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "category": "consequence",
      "text": "The Festival of the Solstice Flame reinforces the Church's doctrine that the Eternal Light is the source of all enduring hope, and that darkness — both literal and spiritual — is temporary.",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
  "names": [
    {
      "name": "Cathedral of the Eternal Light",
      "kind": "place",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "Moskov",
      "kind": "place",
      "aka": ["Moscow"]
    },
    {
      "name": "Patriarch of Moscavia",
      "kind": "lore",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "Synod of Lanterns",
      "kind": "lore",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "Tsar",
      "kind": "lore",
      "aka": []
    },
    {
      "name": "Tsarina",
      "kind": "lore",
      "aka": []
    }
  ],
  "elsewhere": [
    {
      "entry": "Church of the Eternal Light",
      "text": "The Festival of the Solstice Flame is one of the Church's oldest and most widely observed ceremonies, dating back to the founding of the faith in 412 and maintained without interruption ever since.",
      "held_by": null
    },
    {
      "entry": "Parish priest",
      "text": "The festival elevates the parish priest's role in the community, making him the visible link between the central authority of the Church in Moskov and the local Owl-folk families he serves.",
      "held_by": null
    }
  ],
  "part_of": {
    "entry": "Church of the Eternal Light",
    "drawer": "Rites and Ceremonies"
  },
  "move_in": []
}

